Hi,
here's a simple tool to customize SELinux configuration for network
applications.

Here's how it work :
- After un-archiving and decompressing the attach "configTool.tgz" with "tar
-xzvf configTool.tgz" (this should create the "test8" directory in the
current one), you can start the application by calling "./test8/test8.tcl"
from the command line.  It should work on Red Hat 7.x or later (because the
default version of tcl and wish are sufficient).

- I did deactivate the "save", "reload" and "relabel" function.  This means
the application is only an interface that can't arm your system.

- The application goal is: Allowing a system administrator to configure
network applications on a SELinux Box without TE configuration knowledge.

- USAGE:
1.You enter the absolute path of an executable binary file in the "Source
Binary" textbox.

2.For labelled networking or the loopback, you can optionally enter the
absolute path of an executable binary file in the "Target Binary" textbox.

APP : the name of the Source Binary entered will be extract to create (or
eventually open) the binaryFile.te (in policy/domains/program) and
binaryFile.fc (in policy/file_context/program).

3.Choose an object class and a subclass.  And grant the permissions you want
the associate domain.

4.Once done, "save" your configuration, "reload" the SELinux policy or
"relabel" the file system.

APP : the result of the save operation is to write the appropriate
permissions in the binaryFile.fc and binaryFile.te


- You can look at the source code in the test8/test8ifc.tcl file.


Before I go further with the development of this tool, I would like to know
what you think about it on the utility, security, design, ... aspects.


Thank you,
all your comments will be appreciated.

Eric Gingras
